      "name": "FreeMemoryPool",
      "signature": "void FreeMemoryPool(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "comment": "Frees dynamically allocated memory pool and resets global pointers.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Check if global pool pointer (0x0435b6e4) is non-null\n2. Calculate pool size by subtracting pool base from end pointer (0x0435b6ec)\n3. Align size to 4-byte boundary using mask 0xfffffffc\n4. Validate size is at least 0x1000 (4096) bytes\n5. Load allocation header from -4 offset before pool pointer\n6. Calculate metadata offset difference and validate it's <= 0x1f (31 bytes)\n7. Call FreeMemory with memory pointer and size\n8. Zero out global pointers: 0x0435b6e4, 0x0435b6e8, 0x0435b6ec\n9. Return to caller\n\nParameters:\n(none) - Operates on static global memory pool pointers\n\nReturns:\nvoid - No return value\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- If pool pointer is null, function returns immediately without freeing\n- Corruption detection: If metadata offset > 0x1f, calls CorruptionDetected() (non-returning)\n- If aligned size < 0x1000, skips size validation and proceeds to free\n- Magic numbers: 0x1000 = minimum pool size, 0x1f = max metadata offset\n\nStructure Layout:\nThe memory pool uses a header-based allocation scheme where:\n  Offset -4: Contains pointer to actual allocation base (before user data)\n  Offset  0: Start of user-accessible pool data\n  Size varies: Calculated from (end_pointer - pool_pointer) aligned to 4 bytes",
      "name_source": "LoD/PD2",
